
@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Components_Skip_To)]
<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Page_Title_Components_Skip_To</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">@GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Components_Skip_To Component</h1>

<p>
    The Skip To component is designed with two use cases in mind. The first will probably cover 90% of cases, where it simply sits at the top-right corner of the site and always targets the main content element. 
    In the case of Blazor, that main content is most likely fixed, with the <span class="code-span">&#64;Body</span> injecting the content.
</p>
<p>
    Given that a skip to for the site may be on a non-interactive area, the Skip To has been designed to work with or without interactivity at the (<span class="code-span">SkipToType.Site</span>) level.
    Currently, I only support having one Skip to at the Site level which it positioned top (LTR/RTL).  
</p>
<p>
    The skip to given a valid target will scroll to, and set focus on the target. If the user has reduced motion set on their device then rather that a scroll the target will snap into position.
</p>
<p>
    To try the skip to at the Site level (its at the top left of the page), keep pressing the tab key and eventually it will become <span class="code-span">:focus-visible</span>
    Pressing the enter key will then put the focus the on the container that this content is inside.
</p>
<p>
    The second use case is where you have a block of repeated items on each interactive page and need to skip this content i.e. a secondary skip to.
    You can have multiple skip to components on a page at this level (<span class="code-span">SkipToType.Section</span>). Each skip to for a section uses absolute positioning
    relative to its parent (that has a position set, such as relative) and will be position top (LTR/RTL) within its parent.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Note: </b>For either use case as mentioned above don't forget to add tabindex="-1" on the target element.
</p>
<h2 id="skip-to-keyboard" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="skip-to-keyboard">
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> - activates the link and moves focus to the target element.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    There is a skip to inside the section below that will jump to its target, the container of: "The content you wand to get to.". Use your tab key to find it.
</p>
<div class="info-box">
    <SkipTo IconVisible="true" SkipToText="Skip to section content" SkipToType="SkipToType.Section" TargetID="section-one" />
    <div class="section-box">
        The skip to is hiding here in this container that has a CSS <spoan class="code-span">position:relative;</spoan> set on it.
    </div>
    <div class="section-box">
        Some block you want to skip.
    </div>
    <div style="width:320px;" class="section-box" tabindex="-1" id="section-one">
        The content you want to get to.
    </div>
</div>
<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">Installation</h2>

    <p>
        <b>Note: </b> It is a requirement that the Blazor Ramp Core <span class="code-span">core-live-region.js</span> script, <span class="code-span">LiveRegionService</span>, and
        associated <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component are added alongside this component’s specific
        requirements (a stylesheet reference in this instance). You do not need to add the BlazorRamp.Core package separately, as BlazorRamp.Core is included as a transitive package, you only need to add the packages that contain the components
        that you wish to use i.e the <b>BlazorRamp.SkipTo</b> NuGet package.
    </p>
    <p>
        Please follow the instructions in the <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Getting_Started">@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_About - @GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Getting_Started</a>
        ensuring that in the <span class="code-span">&lt;head&gt;</span> section you also add the Skip To stylesheet as follows:
    </p>
    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Add_Skip_To_Style_Sheet" AriaLabel="Add skip to stylesheet code block" />

</section>

<section class="code-table">

    <h2 id="skip-to-component-parameters" class="code-table__heading">SkipTo component parameters</h2>

    <table aria-labelledby="skip-to-component-parameters">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Parameter</th>
                <th scope="col">Type</th>
                <th scope="col">Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>SkipToText</td>
                <td>string</td>
                <td>The text displayed by the skip to link. Defaults to "Skip to content".</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>TargetID</td>
                <td>string</td>
                <td>The ID of the target element to move focus to. Defaults to "main-content".</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>SkipToType</td>
                <td>SkipToType (enum)</td>
                <td>
                    Sets whether the skip link is positioned for the entire page or a specific section.
                    Defaults to <span class="code-span">SkipToType.Site</span>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>IconVisible</td>
                <td>bool</td>
                <td>Indicates whether to display the skip link icon (person running). Defaults to true. </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">Setting component parameters</h2>

    <p>Example showing the parameters being set for the skip to link on this site.</p>

    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Skip_To_Params_Example" AriaLabel="Skip to example code block" />

    <p>
        For something that is site wide <span class="code-span">SkipToType.Site</span>, I just place the component inside the
        <span class="code-span">Routes.razor</span> file or <span class="code-span">App.razor</span> file above the router with the
        <span class="code-span">AnnouncementHistory</span> component.
    </p>

    <p>The following example is the skip to on this page using the <span class="code-span">SkipToType.Section</span></p>

    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Skip_To_Section_Example" AriaLabel="Skip to section example code block" />

</section>

<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">CSS variables defined for the skip to component.</h2>

    <p>
        The following sections outline all of the css variables that can be set to alter the styling of the skip to component.
        You can of course also override its style classes directly.
    </p>

    <section class="code-table">

        <h3 id="skip-to-outline-variables" class="code-table__heading">Skip to outline variables</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="skip-to-outline-variables">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Variable Name</th>
                    <th scope="col">Default Value</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>--br-comp-skip-to-outline-style</td>
                    <td>var(--br-comp-all-outline-style)</td>
                    <td>Style of the outline around the component on focus - uses the shared component variable.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>--br-comp-skip-to-outline-width</td>
                    <td>var(--br-comp-all-outline-width)</td>
                    <td>Width of the outline - uses the shared component variable.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>--br-comp-skip-to-outline-offset</td>
                    <td>var(--br-unit-outline-offset-0)</td>
                    <td>Outline offset - <b>does not use the shared component variable.</b></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>--br-comp-skip-to-outline-colour</td>
                    <td>var(--br-comp-all-outline-colour)</td>
                    <td>The outline colour - uses the shared component variable.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            <b>Note: </b> Given that the Skip To is likely to be placed top left, there was more risk of contrast violations with an offset outline.
            To simulate just the outline and its offset, a border is used that has the same colour as the canvas and then an outline is applied without an offset.
        </p>
    </section>

